## Learned User Preferences

- Credit/account-state chat errors resume via `continueChat()`; billing text classifies as `credits`, never destructive `tool_error` regeneration.
- Supersedes older retry notes: chat-error banners use `Try again` + `continueChat()`; only credits say `Resume`; destructive reroll only via model switch.
- Agent-visible filesystem metadata must preserve required text `lineCount` and binary `contentKind` from provider stats through snapshots, RPC/tools, and UI; never synthesize size-only file entries.
- `libs/chat` holds only cross-package shared schemas (messages, RPC, tool inputs, sentinels) — API-only types like request body envelopes/controller DTOs stay in apps/api (`body` is an API concept).
- Chat Zod schemas stay pure (no `.refine`/`.superRefine`/`.check`) so they convert cleanly to JSON Schema for OpenAPI exposure; centralise validation via `nestjs-zod` `ZodValidationPipe` at the controller boundary, never post-hoc in services.
- Required chat-message metadata (e.g. `kernel`, `testingEnabled`) must be populated at every call-site — API rejects missing/undefined with bad-request rather than silently falling back to defaults.
- Per-chat-response settings (model, temperature, sampling) follow OpenAI-compatible API shape over bespoke Tau wrappers; research Anthropic/OpenAI/Gemini conventions before designing custom request fields.
- Chat wire protocol stays orchestrator-agnostic — reject delta-only payloads; client sends full UI message history each turn so backend orchestrators can be swapped without LangGraph lock-in.
- CAD agent system prompts must explicitly state production-grade, hyper-realistic design expectations and the target audience (architects/engineers) — without that domain context the LLM defaults to "toy" outputs because the simplest path looks acceptable; never rely on assumed business context.
- CAD agent-authored visible labels use Title Case words with spaces; code identifiers keep each kernel's native casing.
- CAD prompts must steer the model to exact mathematical/curve-based geometry over sampled approximations — fold model-self-recognized topology insights into the per-kernel prompt section so it never shortcuts to sampling by default.
- Cross-provider content normalisation belongs at the API middleware layer (e.g. `cross-provider-content-normalizer`), never the UI or `tool-result-trimmer` — provider-specific image/format quirks must be healed centrally and provider-agnostically.
- Media tool results must be classified before text budgeting; screenshots go to trimmer/provider image blocks, never `.txt` persisted-output recovery.
- Prefer API middleware over forking `@langchain/*` / `@langchain/google-*` for cross-provider healing; fork only when upstream has no fix and the user explicitly approves.
- Keep provider-facing tool schemas minimal and implemented-only; never expose internal RPC fields or speculative records, and guard LangChain JSON Schema for Vertex-breaking keywords.
- Never drop persisted reasoning/thinking blocks on cross-provider model switches — accuracy loss is unacceptable; heal via middleware, not v1 omission or stripping.
- Surgical LLM-dep rollbacks: revert only `@langchain/*` / `@ai-sdk/*` versions in the lockfile; keep load-bearing healing middleware and `outputVersion: 'v1'` in `provider.service.ts`.
- Prefer algorithmic, code-level solutions over bundler config or Vite plugins; middleware and transforms must be provider-agnostic (no model/provider-specific branching) — handle all providers generically; text normalization/sanitization AND malformed-payload healing (e.g. interrupted-stream tool inputs that fail strict schema validation) belong at the API layer (middleware/transforms/preprocess), never in UI components — the UI already renders fine without healing and per-render coercion misses every part already persisted in the DB; optimize for 3rd-party consumer DX; CAD export should not hardcode a single interchange format (e.g. GLB-only) or embed `@taucad/converter` as an internal runtime dependency — treat converters and third-party exporters (e.g. API-based format transformers) as injectable plugins with multiple providers allowed concurrently; agent-surface APIs (RPCs, tool inputs, system prompts) require explicit parameters with no silent `?? mainEntryPath`-style fallbacks so the LLM must commit to the file/target it intends; cross-cutting agent UI metadata (e.g. reasoning timing) namespaced under the generic `common` AI-SDK provider key — not a per-product `taucad` key — and typed end-to-end via `MyUIMessage` schemas (never `unknown` providerMetadata)

## Learned Workspace Facts

- `RpcFileSystem` uses project-relative keys (`''` root); runtime and DeepAgents adapters alone translate to their strict absolute namespaces.
- Runtime-owned nested chat schemas derive from runtime registries (e.g. `kernelIssueCodeValues`) and preserve `details`/frame context; never hand-write drift-prone enums.
- `.agents/skills/*` is the canonical editor-agnostic skill location; `.tau/skills` is legacy fallback only, and activation goes through `use_skill`.
- `repos/claude-code` is the canonical reference for tool-call API simplicity and context-engineering patterns (offloading, RPC reuse) — consult before adding new tool-call API surface.
- OpenAI compat for chat APIs splits into D1 (per-request config at top-level body field e.g. `agent`) vs D2-D4 (message shape, endpoint rename, body envelope); only D1 is load-bearing — defer the rest.
- Vertex Gemini implicit context caching is enabled by default (not 'no cache') — async promotion lag means only the early prefix sticks (~85% hit); explicit `cachedContent` needed for ~99%.
- Round-trip stable `msg_` ids through `toBaseMessages` into LangGraph BaseMessages is P0 — `@ai-sdk/langchain` currently strips them, breaking `messagesStateReducer` dedup.
- Compaction must be committed via LangGraph state rewrite plus checkpoint-aware request merge; provider-call-local compaction is a bug.
- Chat compaction clears read_file dedup via explicit ReadDedupClearer; never infer sidecar cleanup through LangGraph runtime stores or BaseStore.search.
- Client is truth-bearer for visible history — API reconciles LangGraph to client UIMessages by id; server owns middleware, byte-exact reminders, and LangGraph persistence.
- Cancel semantics: keep partial assistant only if client history includes it; edit/resubmit a prior user message removes that turn plus any subsequent partial assistant from server state.
- Backend-injected reminders, token-usage, and file snapshots persist byte-exactly in LangGraph state — drop server bookkeeping only when its anchoring user turn is removed.
- OpenAI's vision API silently fails on persisted base64 images mid-conversation (Anthropic accepts them) — repaired by re-emitting images via `cross-provider-content-normalizer.middleware.ts` (Path A) before model dispatch; never patch in tool-result trimmer or UI.
- Provider-visible `additional_kwargs` is provider-owned replay metadata; canonicalize/drop legacy `tool_calls`/`function_call` at the API boundary before model calls.
- Streaming tool-call ids from provider forks must be unique per model invocation and stable only across chunks for the same call; never derive ids from name+partIndex.
- LangChain Google fork rebases require Gemini parallel and same-name tool replay tests; fork only `google-common` unless tests prove wrappers/auth need it.
- Gemini streaming adapters must bind every `partialArgs` fragment to id/index/name before LangChain core; never emit anonymous argument-bearing `tool_call_chunk`s.
- Read-file line-number gutter prefixing belongs in the rendering layer, not the stored tool content — embedding line numbers in `tool-edit-tests.ts` storage breaks edit/replace pipelines.
- Tool input schemas for AI chat live in `libs/chat/src/schemas/tool-input.registry.ts` as a single registry keyed by tool name (`toolInputSchemas`/`getToolInputSchema`) and form the single source of truth for "is this input legal for this tool?"; used exclusively by the API-side preprocess in `libs/chat/src/schemas/message.schema.ts` to detect persisted `output-error` tool parts whose `input` no longer satisfies the per-tool strict schema (typically because the LLM stream was interrupted before the input finished serialising) and demote the malformed value into `rawInput` so `convertToModelMessages` → `messageContentSanitizerMiddleware` can pair a synthetic `tool_result` with the dangling `tool_use` block; UI never coerces or heals tool inputs (deliberately kept server-side only — UI renders fine without it and would otherwise miss every part already persisted in IndexedDB/Postgres); the contract is documented in `docs/policy/interrupted-tool-call-contract.md` and tested end-to-end via `apps/api/app/api/chat/interrupted-tool-roundtrip.test.ts` (real provider calls across anthropic/gemini/gpt), `apps/api/app/testing/interrupted-tool-providers.integration.test.ts`, and `apps/api/app/api/chat/middleware/message-content-sanitizer.middleware.test.ts` — never relax the strict schema at the pipe; always normalize partial inputs through the registry preprocess instead. `test.json` is per-file shape `{ [sourcePath]: { requirements: [] } }` so a project tests multiple geometry units concurrently — `targetFile` is required (no `?? mainEntryPath` fallback) on every agent-surface RPC and tool input (`fetch_geometry`, `capture_screenshot`, `test_model`, `edit_tests`, `get_kernel_result`); per-geometry-unit geometry artifacts use `${toolCallId}__${slug(targetFile)}.glb`; `rpcClientErrorCode` in `libs/chat/src/schemas/rpc.schema.ts` is the single catalog for RPC wire client error codes (UI imports it from `@taucad/chat`; do not recreate parallel UI error-code maps); reasoning timing is stateless server-side via `providerMetadata.common.reasoningStartedAtMs` on each `reasoning-start` part keyed by reasoning-block `id` (correlates interleaved thinking blocks across one message), surfaced via `MyUIMessage` typed schema — UI renders 'Thought briefly' (<1s) or 'Thought for Xs' from start/end timestamp diff (no client stopwatch, no clock-skew compensation); `agent-safeguards.middleware.ts` (`apps/api/app/api/chat/middleware/`) detects repeated identical tool failures (3 **sequential** errors on the same tool+input across consecutive turns) and injects a one-shot guidance message into the latest tool result steering the LLM to alternative actions — appended to the existing tool result so it does not invalidate Anthropic prompt cache; parallel/fan-out tool calls within a single assistant turn must NEVER trip the safeguard even when the same tool name appears multiple times (e.g. `create_file` invoked four ways in one fan-out is one parallel intent, not four sequential failed retries) — keying on tool+input alone is insufficient, the detector must require sequential turns AND distinct error events to avoid the false-positive that pushes the LLM to abandon legitimate parallel workloads; full system-prompt audit at `docs/research/system-prompt-audit.md` follows the gap-analysis checklist style of `filesystem-gap-analysis.md`; system prompts and tool-call descriptions use "geometry unit" everywhere — the "geometry unit" acronym is reserved for internal code only; kernel-specific test-failure recovery guidance lives inside each kernel's section of the system prompt (never as unconditional generic text that would mislead when paired with another kernel — e.g. don't reference OpenSCAD entry-point conventions in the Replicad section); each kernel ships a canonical multi-file example under `apps/api/app/api/chat/prompts/kernel-prompt-configs/<kernel>.prompt.example-multifile/` (jscad/manifold/opencascadejs/openscad/replicad/zoo) that the system prompt embeds verbatim to demonstrate idiomatic module decomposition; OpenSCAD multi-file examples MUST use `use <lib/file.scad>` (imports modules/functions only, no top-level execution) — `include <lib/file.scad>` inlines the entire file including any geometry calls at module scope, producing a duplicate copy of imported objects in the output (smoking-gun "robot rendered twice" symptom in early OpenSCAD examples); `test_model` dynamically creates the geometry unit on demand rather than asking the LLM to bootstrap — missing-file errors stay legitimate failures, not pre-bootstrapping requests; testing-related prompts push the LLM to add a top-level kernel-specific export so a file becomes testable rather than to skip writing the test; cross-provider thinking-block portability — Anthropic's `{type:"thinking", thinking, signature}` blocks are persisted verbatim by LangGraph's `PostgresSaver`, then crash on the next turn when the user switches to Gemini/Vertex because `@langchain/google-common`'s `messageContentComplexToPart` only handles `text|image_url|media|reasoning|input_audio` — fix by setting `outputVersion: "v1"` on `ChatAnthropic` (and `ChatVertexAI` once the fork picks up the upstream Gemini PRs) so `convertToV1FromAnthropicMessage` normalizes outgoing AIMessages to standard `{type:"reasoning", reasoning, signature}` blocks (`langchain-anthropic`'s `_formatStandardContent` re-emits as `thinking + signature` only when `response_metadata.model_provider === "anthropic"`, dropping the signature on cross-provider hops automatically); backstop existing checkpoint corpus with `crossProviderContentNormalizerMiddleware` wired before `messageContentSanitizerMiddleware` (rewrite legacy `thinking → reasoning`, drop `signature` when target ≠ source provider, wrap `redacted_thinking`/`compaction` in `{type:"non_standard"}`); never strip thinking blocks unconditionally, never branch in UI, never disable thinking — keep the normalization in middleware; `export_geometry` chat tool writes the exported bytes to the in-app filesystem and is categorised as `write` in `assistant-message-activity.ts` so it renders **outside** the `Explored…` research aggregate (top-level row, not folded) — research summaries stay `files → searches → fetches → renders → screenshots → tests` with no `exports` segment; the success row pairs a `Download` outline button with an `Open Exporter` link button (`chat-message-tool-export-geometry.tsx`) that sends `editorRef.send({ type: 'setPanelState', panel: 'converter', mobileActiveTab: 'converter' })` via `useProject()` — the same path as `project-command-items.tsx` Export. RPC type-safety: `RpcCall<K extends keyof RpcSchemasRegistry>` (`libs/chat/src/schemas/rpc.schema.ts`) + `RpcRequest<K>` (`libs/chat/src/types/websocket.types.ts`) + `RpcDispatcher.dispatch<K>` backed by `RpcHandlerMap` replace the prior switch/`rpcRequestToCallInput` wire utility (deleted) — headless paths use `dispatch(call)` with no `as` casts, but a tsgo correlated-union limitation means re-boxing `{rpcName, args}` from a discriminated `RpcRequest` union breaks correlation, so a single documented `as RpcCall<C['rpcName']>` cast remains at the `createRpcHandlers` browser-adapter boundary (intentional, not a design failure). `@ai-sdk/langchain` is pinned via `patches/@ai-sdk__langchain@<version>.patch` (currently `2.0.181`, bump the patch filename when upgrading — pnpm patch naming) so multi-turn GPT-5.5 / GPT-5.3 Codex chats no longer leak prior reasoning blocks into the live UI as a stray `Thought briefly` header — regression covered by `apps/api/app/testing/aisdk-langchain-values-reasoning-leak.test.ts` (annotates the LangGraph stream as `AsyncIterable<readonly unknown[]>` and casts `toUIMessageStream(...)` to `AsyncIterable<AIMessageChunk>` to bridge the LangGraph tuple-stream / AI-SDK package-type gap); langchain-google forks (`@taucad/langchain-google-common`, `@taucad/langchain-google-vertexai`) are rebased on upstream and repacked as `tarballs/langchain-fork/langchain-google-{common,vertexai}-<version>.tgz` — bump both tgz versions in lockstep and validate the gemini live e2e integration tests after each rebase. Stack is on LangChain/LangGraph 1.2 — build new agent primitives (e.g. `wrapModelCall`/`wrapToolCall` middleware, `BaseCallbackHandler` substrate) on top of v1.2 rather than backporting to older APIs. Parallel-tool incremental persistence: LangGraph's `Send` v2 fan-out gives parallel tool calls independent tasks with per-task pending writes (server-side durability is correct), but the **client-side `chat-session-store.ts` only calls `queuePersist` on the AI-SDK terminal `onFinish` callback** — if the tab closes/navigates or the API rolls mid-stream, every successful tool result that landed in `Chat.messages` is lost; `finalizeInterruptedToolParts` makes it worse on retry by demoting any non-`output-available` tool part to `output-error: USER_INTERRUPTED` without consulting an RPC ledger, so the LLM re-issues tool calls that already succeeded; the API also rebuilds LangGraph state from the request body (UI's IndexedDB tail), which can override the per-task pending writes the checkpointer correctly recorded — the recommended fix wires `~registerMessagesCallback` to `queuePersist` on milestone parts (`tool-output-available`, `tool-output-error`, `text-end`, `reasoning-end`) plus an RPC ledger consulted by `finalizeInterruptedToolParts`; Cline's "exactly one tool per assistant message" workaround is rejected (kills parallelism, breaks the prompt-cache prefix, fights every other Tau system-prompt section) — full design captured in `docs/research/parallel-tool-call-incremental-persistence.md`. Parallel-tool output interleaving: LangGraph's superstep model batches every fan-out tool's `tool-output-available` at superstep end (every Δ≈0ms across the parallel group), so the UI flips all loading spinners to "complete" simultaneously even though each tool's RPC finished at a different time — the goal is Cursor-style per-tool emission where each `tool-output-available` fires as soon as its own RPC returns (the test contract is captured by `apps/api/app/testing/parallel-tool-call.integration.test.ts`, which asserts each write's RPC fires immediately on its own `tool-input-available` and outputs interleave per-tool); rejected approaches enumerated in `docs/research/parallel-tool-output-interleaving.md`: a `TAU_LANGGRAPH_TOOL_CONCURRENCY` knob (wrong axis — drop the env var entirely), enforced inter-tool gaps or RPC sequencing (we want parallel RPCs, not serialised ones), validating partial JSON until it parses (optional fields silently skipped when they hadn't streamed yet), and an `EagerDispatchCallbackHandler` that hardcodes which tools own RPCs (couples tool-domain knowledge into the dispatcher — the architecturally correct trigger is the tool's own logic firing on `tool-input-available` so no extra layer needs to know which tools have RPCs); the solution must work WITH LangChain (no tricks, no fighting the framework — reframe the eigenquestion when proposed options all overengineer the problem). Eager tool dispatch architecture (POC GO verdict, `apps/api/app/api/chat/eager-dispatch/` + `docs/research/eager-tool-dispatch-poc.md`): a `BaseCallbackHandler` attached to `createAgent({...})` invocation receives `handleLLMNewToken` with populated `tool_call_chunks` during streaming and a final `handleLLMEnd` carrying parsed `AIMessage.tool_calls[]`; trigger eager `tool.invoke()` from S2 (index advance in `tool_call_chunks` for non-last tools) + S3 (`handleLLMEnd` for the last tool); `wrapToolCall` middleware short-circuits subsequent native `ToolNode` execution to return the cached eager result; the LangGraph `'custom'` writer must be captured explicitly from `request.runtime` inside a `wrapModelCall` hook and threaded into the handler — `getWriter()` (`AsyncLocalStorage`-backed) does NOT propagate reliably into `BaseCallbackHandler.handleLLMNewToken`; test ordering uses stream-relative assertions (compare against `lastBlock1ChunkTime`) rather than wall-clock thresholds (`t < 250ms`) to eliminate flake. Resumable chat streams (`docs/research/resumable-chat-streams.md`): `chat-error-service-unavailable.tsx`'s "Retry" button currently calls `useChatActions().regenerate()` → AI SDK `chat.regenerate()`, which slices off the trailing assistant message and wipes every part the user just watched stream in (file edits, thinking, tool calls) — this is the smoking-gun "I lost all my work" UX bug; the per-message `Try again` hover-footer affordance must KEEP its regenerate semantics (deliberate "regenerate from scratch with a fresh model"); the network-error banner must instead be a continuation (R1) plus transparent transport-level auto-retry with a Codex-style `Reconnecting... N/M` indicator that only surfaces the banner after the auto-retry budget is exhausted (R2); page-reload-tolerant `Chat.resumeStream()` + AI-SDK `resumable-stream` + Redis is R3 (deferred). `export_geometry` chat tool gating: system prompt instructs the agent to invoke `export_geometry` only when the user explicitly asks to export (the agent was over-eagerly exporting after every render); use POSITIVE wording per `docs/policy/context-engineering-policy.md` ("invoke when explicitly asked") rather than negative "do not export unless".
- Skill listing/content spans the browser↔server boundary: the listing (name/description/**fingerprint**) is computed client-side and rides `contextPayload.skills` per message; skill **content** is cached server-side in the `recent_skills` LangGraph store and re-injected post-compaction with a "do not re-read the filesystem" directive. The per-skill `fingerprint` (canonical djb2 `hashString` from `@taucad/utils/hash`, identical on both sides) is the cross-boundary cache-invalidation token — `recent-skills.middleware.ts` reconciles each stored fingerprint against the current listing and evicts (`BaseStore.delete` + `__index` rewrite) on mismatch/absence, so stale content is never re-injected under the no-re-read directive. Never key a skill cache on `chatId` (unrelated to content); make the client listing reactive via `treeService.subscribeTree` (`usePromptSkillsCatalog` mirrors `useSkillsCatalog`) instead of freezing per chat. Design + status: `docs/research/skill-cache-invalidation-topology.md`.
- Chat filesystem time-travel = materialize-from-snapshots, not diff-reversal: every create/edit/delete `diffStats` persists full before/after content and the token trimmer strips only the LLM-bound copy, so any prior state is reconstructable by replaying ops to a cutoff. Stamp assistant `createdAt` in `persistMessagesActor` (the persist choke point) for the cross-chat ordering key; `delete_file`/`create_file` must capture pre-mutation content in one RPC to stay invertible. Restore writes go back through the normal `source:'machine'` fs path. Design + impl: `docs/research/chat-restore-time-travel.md`.
- Persist `Project` fields only through the project machine: `writeProjectActor` does a full-document replace (`indexeddb-storage.ts` `isProject` branch), so any field written out-of-band goes stale in `context.project` and is clobbered + reverted by a `projectUpdated` read-back. The `revisionState` restore pointer regressed exactly this way (direct `projectManager.updateProject` + a `projectUpdated → HYDRATE` seam) — restores reverted on every projectUpdated until refresh, plus a partial-write vs full-replace DB race. Fix routes persistence through an `updateRevisionState` event (single writer, atomic across every surface); never write a `Project` field out-of-band. Design: `docs/research/revision-state-atomic-persistence.md`.
- Revision/turn identity keys on the user-message **`id`**, never a `createdAt`/`anchor` timestamp — timestamps collide (absent/duplicate `createdAt`, same-ms, persist-time stamping) and drift across persist/reload, which dropped the first turn's marker and stranded the top-bar chip at "Revision 0 · baseline" while a Revision existed. `buildRevisions` slices ops into turns by `FileOp.turnMessageId` (ownership, not a timestamp window, so a collision can never fold/drop a turn); `useRevisions` resolves the head by `headTurnId` (`''` = tip sentinel → latest, unknown id → falls back to latest, NEVER `undefined`/baseline while Revisions exist); `PersistedRevisionState.restorePoint: number` became `headTurnId: string` with `migrateHeadTurnId` back-compat (legacy anchor → Revision id, else tip). `anchor`/`createdAt` are display/sort keys only. The machine's `latestAnchor` and the numeric `useRestoreToPoint` surface are deleted; `canReturnToLatest` is n-based via `useRevisions`. `stampAssistantCreatedAt` → `stampMessageCreatedAt` now stamps any unstamped row at the persist choke point. Design + status: `docs/research/revision-anchor-identity-collapse.md`.
- File-edit syntax is selected per exact model, but every profile plans and commits through the same client-side filesystem authority.
